what is Simon Says?

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.550
SANDEEP DINESH: Yes, so Simon
Says, or Simon as it's called,

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.884
is a game from the '70s or '80s.

00:04:55.884 --> 00:04:57.800
I'm not sure why in the
'70s-- before my time.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: '79,
'78, I think it came out.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.600
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so
it's a pretty simple game.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.340
It's a box, and it has
four different buttons

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.950
with four different colors.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.770
And it will light up one color,
and then it's a memory game,

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.276
so you press that color.

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:11.650
And then it lights
up two colors,

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:12.610
and you press those two colors.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.140
And then three, and then
four, and then as many as you

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.360
can until you lose, right?

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.320
There's no winning this game.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.153
It's a great--

00:05:19.153 --> 00:05:20.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's
no winning this game.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.710
SANDEEP DINESH: Great game
that you can only lose.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.293
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it was all--
this is basically my childhood

00:05:24.293 --> 00:05:27.260
before there were things like
Sega or Nintendo or anything.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:28.450
I was like, OK red.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:29.270
Red, yellow.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:30.140
Red, yellow, red.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.720
Red, yellow, red, blue.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:32.920
And so on so forth.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.280
SANDEEP DINESH: Hours
of entertainment.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
why are we talking about it?

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:43.220
MARK MANDEL: So Sandeep and I
were talking at a conference--

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:44.920
oh, wow, about a year
ago at least now.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.000
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah,
almost a year ago.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.610
MARK MANDEL: --called Ubiquity,
which was an IoT conference.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.290
And we got pushed together into
building a project together.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.190
And we came up with
this idea for Simon,

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.330
but we wanted to make
Simon multiplayer

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.550
and massively
scalable multiplayer.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.050
SANDEEP DINESH: So
it's interesting.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.249
I think I was going
to give an IoT talk,

00:06:03.249 --> 00:06:05.290
and then you were going
to give a container talk.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:06.950
And then we're like, let's do--

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:07.470
MARK MANDEL: Together!

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:08.970
SANDEEP DINESH: A
big talk together.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.505
And then we said, what can we
do with containers, and IoT,

00:06:11.505 --> 00:06:15.330
and real-time, and all
these buzzwordy stuff?

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.512
Can we do something that
puts it all together, right?

00:06:18.512 --> 00:06:20.470
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so we
came up with the idea.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.678
I think, actually, you came
up with the idea-- credit

00:06:22.678 --> 00:06:25.700
where credit's due-- of
taking Simon Says and saying,

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:28.760
OK, let's make this
playable over the internet.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.700
So just to understand before we
get on how you actually build

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.640
this, how does one play it?

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.324
What is the mechanics
of the game?

00:06:36.324 --> 00:06:37.990
SANDEEP DINESH: The
multiplayer version?

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:38.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:39.490
SANDEEP DINESH:
Yeah, so let's say me

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.390
and Mark are playing, right.

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:42.850
I will say, green.

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.160
So I'll pick the first
color, and I'll pick green.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.670
Now on Mark's device or
screen, it'll show up green.

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:52.150
And so now he presses green, and
then he picks the next color.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.846
So it would be
green, yellow, right?

00:06:54.846 --> 00:06:55.720
MARK MANDEL: Exactly.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:56.770
And then, so I send it to you.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:57.635
SANDEEP DINESH: And then
it comes to me, and then

00:06:57.635 --> 00:06:58.950
on my screen green, yellow.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.140
And then I can go
green, yellow, red.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:01.556
And then it goes
to Mark's screen.

00:07:01.556 --> 00:07:04.580
And so we just keep
adding an additional color

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:05.490
until someone loses.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.719
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
OK, so it is online,

00:07:08.719 --> 00:07:09.760
and there's many players.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.877
But in any given match,
there's only two players.

00:07:12.877 --> 00:07:13.585
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:07:13.585 --> 00:07:14.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, OK.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.030
So why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:18.320
about how you built this.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:19.945
We're on Google Cloud
Platform Podcast,

00:07:19.945 --> 00:07:21.772
so I'm assuming that
the Cloud is involved?

00:07:21.772 --> 00:07:23.230
SANDEEP DINESH:
Yeah, a little bit.

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:23.530
Little bit.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:24.730
MARK MANDEL: Little bit.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.145
SANDEEP DINESH:
Surprisingly, a little bit.

00:07:27.145 --> 00:07:29.770
MARK MANDEL: So first thing that
we decided we were going to do

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.130
was to talk about how we
wanted this to communicate.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.210
Like Sandeep said, real-time
is really important.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.011
So if I press the color
red on my machine,

00:07:37.011 --> 00:07:38.510
it should show up
red on his machine

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:40.820
in relatively real-time.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.187
We don't want latency
to be an issue.

00:07:43.187 --> 00:07:44.520
What other criteria did we have?

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.728
I think security was a big
concern for us, obviously.

00:07:46.728 --> 00:07:47.890
Hacking Simon Says.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:48.560
SANDEEP DINESH: Simon
Says is the number

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:49.610
one most hacked game.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:50.110
It's a--

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:50.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's a big issue.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:51.660
MARK MANDEL: That's
the a big issue.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:52.450
SANDEEP DINESH:
A very big issue.

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:54.271
We also wanted
multiple devices to be

00:07:54.271 --> 00:07:56.020
able to support Simon
Says because there's

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:58.080
an IoT aspect to it.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.929
So it has to work
on physical devices.

00:07:59.929 --> 00:08:01.220
We wanted it to work on phones.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:02.761
We wanted it to work
on the computer.

00:08:02.761 --> 00:08:03.970
It has to work in the cloud.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.053
It has to work on all of
these different platforms

00:08:06.053 --> 00:08:07.509
in a seamless experience, right?

00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:09.050
If I'm playing on
the phone, I should

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:10.780
be able to play someone
on an IoT device.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.062
I shouldn't be locked
into playing only someone

00:08:13.062 --> 00:08:14.020
else on a phone, right.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.490
So that was another
big criteria for us.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:17.290
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I think
it's actually interesting,

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:18.200
because I wanted
to build something

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.260
on Android because hardware
stuff terrifies me.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.280
And I think I'm going to set
myself on fire, whereas you

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:24.500
wanted to build this crazy
box with buttons and wires

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:25.600
and things.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.141
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is interesting.

00:08:27.141 --> 00:08:28.960
So you're using
different clients.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.610
One is an IoT stuff,
which I've actually seen.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:35.929
It looks like your normal lunch
bucket with buttons on top.

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.470
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so that's
actually a really long story.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:42.679
Originally, I went and
bought a real Simon Says

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.370
from the '80s from the store.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:47.470
And I took it apart,
and I soldered it,

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.950
and I put Ardruino in it.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.650
And it was so unreliable
that I eventually

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:53.740
ended up drilling
buttons into it

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:57.220
because who knew that the old
Simon Says used actual buttons?

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.340
The new one uses these touch
sensitive capacitive things

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.020
that are not reliable at all.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:05.050
And eventually it turned to a
lunch box with buttons, yes.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:09.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice, so
one runs Android with Java.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:11.144
The other one is running--

00:09:11.144 --> 00:09:12.560
SANDEEP DINESH:
Node.js, actually.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Node.js
on what platform?

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:16.038
SANDEEP DINESH: So
we're actually running

00:09:16.038 --> 00:09:17.342
an Arduino, which runs C code.

00:09:17.342 --> 00:09:20.420
It's talking a protocol called
Fermata, which is basically,

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.450
you can send commands up and
down a serial port to USB.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.919
And then a computer
runs the Node.js code

00:09:25.919 --> 00:09:27.710
and actually communicates
with the Arduino.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.212
So this can be a Raspberry
Pi or a fully fledged laptop.

00:09:30.212 --> 00:09:32.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, what
other clients do we have?

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.695
Do we have iOS or--

00:09:35.695 --> 00:09:37.820
MARK MANDEL: So actually,
when we built this thing,

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:40.460
we've had several team
members commit to it, as well.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.597
So not only is there a
Java Android one, one

00:09:42.597 --> 00:09:44.930
of our teammates, [? Mette, ?]
built a Java command line

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:45.420
client.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.890
So you can interact
within the command line.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:47.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:47.900
MARK MANDEL: And
I think, Sandeep,

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:49.510
you built the Node
web one, as well.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:50.926
SANDEEP DINESH:
Right, so we built

00:09:50.926 --> 00:09:53.615
one that works on the
web with the Node server,

00:09:53.615 --> 00:09:54.490
which is pretty cool.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:55.406
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:55.406 --> 00:09:56.930
So the interesting
thing now is OK,

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.560
so you have all of these
different custon-- all

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.070
of these different
clients, right,

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.270
that are using
different technologies.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:06.220
How do you make them
communicate correctly?

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.190
How do you do this?

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:08.110
MARK MANDEL: So it's
probably also worth

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:09.860
noting that the backend
servers is written

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.240
in your favorite language.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:11.370
SANDEEP DINESH: Python.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:12.080
MARK MANDEL: Written in Go.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:13.420
SANDEEP DINESH: Oh, sorry.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:16.570
MARK MANDEL: So, yeah, I
also wrote the backend server

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:18.220
to allow for all
the matchmaking,

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.100
putting players together so
that they could play games

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.320
together, as well as the
game code in and of itself.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.600
And these all talk seamlessly
with all the clients,

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.870
which is pretty cool.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:28.480
SANDEEP DINESH: Yep, so I
think we have a few choices.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:30.850
We could have used web
sockets, or Firebase, or things

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:31.730
like that.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:35.170
But we ended up picking gRPC
because it had the widest

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.120
array of support for
all these clients

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.560
without having to deal
with a bunch of hacks.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:41.770
And I think it was just
very stable and surprisingly

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.880
easy to get it to all
work, even though we're

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:45.880
programming in four or
five different languages.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:48.213
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, we've done
previous episodes on gRPC,

00:10:48.213 --> 00:10:51.087
but the cool thing about gRPC,
if you haven't listened to it,

00:10:51.087 --> 00:10:53.170
is you write this thing
called a proto file, which

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:56.080
defines the data that goes up
and down the stream, and also

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:56.730
the services.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.660
So basically these RPCs,
or remote procedure

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:00.946
calls that you can
call, and then it

00:11:00.946 --> 00:11:02.320
generates a bunch
of code for you

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.653
that's idiomatic to the
language that you're writing in.

00:11:04.653 --> 00:11:06.509
So whether it's
Node or Go or Java,

00:11:06.509 --> 00:11:08.050
and what's really
cool about that is,

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:09.600
yes, Sandeep and I sat down.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.530
We were like, all right,
how does this work?

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:13.030
Let's write our
proto file, work out

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:15.446
how this thing works, and then
once we had the proto file,

00:11:15.446 --> 00:11:17.910
we just split up and went off
and built our own clients.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.660
SANDEEP DINESH: You
can't put us in a room

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.119
for more than 20 minutes without
us killing each other, so.

00:11:22.119 --> 00:11:23.618
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, this is going

00:11:23.618 --> 00:11:25.450
to be a hard episode to record.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.150
So the next question
is, obviously,

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:29.750
since you're using
protocol buffers,

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.750
one of the coolest things
about protocol buffers

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:35.730
is that it actually uses HTTP/2,
so it makes things way faster.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.620
Is this something
that was going on that

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:41.345
worked in every single of the
platforms that you were using?

00:11:41.345 --> 00:11:42.220
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.350
I think I've done
demos where I've

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:48.310
given this over 2G
connections because you're

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:50.099
in the middle of
somewhere, and you

00:11:50.099 --> 00:11:52.390
don't have a WiFi connection
to your phone or something

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:53.322
like that.

00:11:53.322 --> 00:11:55.030
And it's worked
surprisingly well, right.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.350
Every time you
press that button,

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.766
it goes all the way to the
server in a Google data center,

00:11:58.766 --> 00:12:01.840
and then comes all the way back
on another different internet

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.520
connection to the other device.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.509
And it's almost instantaneous,
which is pretty cool.

00:12:06.509 --> 00:12:08.800
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, we actually
we deliberately wrote it

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:10.150
that way to make
life easier for us,

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.524
and also because
it's a cool demo

00:12:11.524 --> 00:12:14.021
to have it-- once you click
a button for it to light up,

00:12:14.021 --> 00:12:15.520
it actually gets a
light up message.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:16.810
So there's a button
press message

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.570
that goes all way back
and then a light up

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:20.071
message that comes
back again, which

00:12:20.071 --> 00:12:21.778
is not to say you have
to do it that way,

00:12:21.778 --> 00:12:23.030
but that's just how we did it.

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:24.670
And yeah, I've done similar
things, where it's like,

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:25.780
yeah, I've got a
mobile connection,

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.030
and I just push the
button, and it comes back.

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:30.155
You're talking hundreds
milliseconds, if that.

00:12:30.155 --> 00:12:32.530
SANDEEP DINESH: I think the
gRPC bidirectional streaming,

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.574
the latency's very low,
which is pretty nice for us.

00:12:35.574 --> 00:12:37.240
MARK MANDEL: And it's
a binary protocol,

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.190
so it's really tiny going
over the wire, which

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:39.820
makes things great.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:40.630
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah,
we're sending a few bytes,

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:41.712
right, just the color.

00:12:41.712 --> 00:12:44.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So let's talk
about that a little bit more

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.750
because-- so there's
many ways you can do it

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.950
so the communication
is as fast as possible.

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:50.380
And the obvious
one, since you're

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:54.070
having IoT devices that do not
necessarily have a public IP,

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:54.960
could be the polling.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:56.710
But you mentioned
bidirectional streaming.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:58.335
Could you talk a
little bit about that?

00:12:58.335 --> 00:13:01.127
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so gRPC,
one of the really cool things,

00:13:01.127 --> 00:13:02.960
I think, that me and
Mark really like a lot,

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:04.600
is the bidirectional streaming.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:07.417
So with this, you can
send, asynchronously,

00:13:07.417 --> 00:13:10.000
from the client to the server
or from the server to the client

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:14.320
without having to do polling
or any of these typical very

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:15.640
compute heavy tasks, right.

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:17.152
So it's very, very lightweight.

00:13:17.152 --> 00:13:18.610
And the really cool
thing, I think,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:21.644
about the bidirectional stream
is once that stream is open,

00:13:21.644 --> 00:13:23.560
you don't have to send
headers back and forth.

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:26.500
You just send the raw
data, and because it's one

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:29.070
canonical stream, it's easy.

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:30.570
MARK MANDEL: It's really easy.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:31.960
SANDEEP DINESH: I don't know
what the word for it is.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:32.630
MARK MANDEL: No, easy is great.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:35.088
No, but and I think it's-- that
is one of the things I like

00:13:35.088 --> 00:13:35.620
about gRPC.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.260
And the flexibility
that you have there,

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.916
you could just send
synchronous RPC calls.

00:13:40.916 --> 00:13:42.040
I send you some parameters.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:43.930
You send me back
a response, yay.

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.180
But then you can stream
data backwards and forwards.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:48.471
And for something like this,
where we're making a game,

00:13:48.471 --> 00:13:50.030
it's such a nice fit.

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:52.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool,
so now we can understand

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:53.950
how all the clients work.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:54.910
What about the servers?

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:57.055
We said it was running Node.js?

00:13:57.055 --> 00:13:58.930
SANDEEP DINESH: No, the
server is running Go.

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:00.805
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Ah
sorry, it was running Go.

00:14:00.805 --> 00:14:02.281
I'm mixing my stuff already.

00:14:02.281 --> 00:14:02.780
So--

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.000
SANDEEP DINESH: I know you
love Node.js, Francesc.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I know, I love
it so much, almost as much as,

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:08.380
yeah.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:12.190
So what platform was it running?

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:13.800
Was it App Engine,
Compute Engine?

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.894
MARK MANDEL: So
we went with GKE.

00:14:15.894 --> 00:14:16.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:17.350
MARK MANDEL: So--

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:18.040
SANDEEP DINESH:
Container Engine.

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:19.415
SANDEEP DINESH:
Container Engine,

00:14:19.415 --> 00:14:20.975
for a few reasons,
one of which is

00:14:20.975 --> 00:14:23.920
gRPC's supported on Kubernetes.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.310
But it also allows us to scale
nicely, because containers

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.860
in Kubernetes.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:32.080
It worked out really well,
so it's actually noting.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:34.121
We never actually sat
down, and we were like, oh,

00:14:34.121 --> 00:14:37.660
there's a bug with data
going from Node to Go

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.510
or-- that was never an issue.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:41.440
That never-- it was
always like, oh, there's

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.260
something wrong with
my code or something.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:44.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:46.540
SANDEEP DINESH:
Yeah, we were using

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.180
Redis readings for the caching
layer, for the persistence,

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:49.680
right.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:52.510
And that works perfectly in
Kubernetes, very easy to scale.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.590
And then our Go code
that you wrote also

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.370
can scale up and down.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.980
And it's very easy because
we used the TCP load balancer

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.425
on Google Cloud so that
gRPC connection just finds

00:15:02.425 --> 00:15:03.720
the server and connects to it.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:04.180
It's pretty straightforward.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:05.555
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so you can run

00:15:05.555 --> 00:15:08.360
five instances of the server,
2,000, 200, whatever you like.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:10.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, so let's talk

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.360
a little bit about the Redis
instances that you had.

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:14.320
How many did you have?

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:15.270
Did you have one?

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.728
Did you have it
run in a container?

00:15:16.728 --> 00:15:19.270
MARK MANDEL: For demo
purposes, I just run the one.

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:20.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Was it
running on a container?

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:22.576
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so I run
it, one, as a container.

00:15:22.576 --> 00:15:25.570
There's no reason why you
couldn't have a cluster.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:27.280
Basically, we use it
for some persistence

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:30.280
and basically for inner
container communication.

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:32.574
There's a [INAUDIBLE]
mechanism inside Redis.

00:15:32.574 --> 00:15:34.240
So if you started a
game-- so basically,

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.010
if one player is connected
to one container,

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:37.830
one player is connected
to another container,

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:39.630
it allows that information
to flow between them,

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:40.770
say this is what's happening.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:41.200
SANDEEP DINESH:
Yeah, and we could

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:43.150
have used data store,
or pub/sub, or something

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:43.649
like that.

00:15:43.649 --> 00:15:45.610
But I think another
goal of this project

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:47.650
was to keep it 100%
open source, right.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:49.190
So that was very
important for us.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.440
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and that
worked that worked really,

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:51.939
really well.

00:15:51.939 --> 00:15:54.760
But yeah, so you could run a
huge Redis cluster if you want.

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:55.950
We keep it really simple.

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:57.520
I don't even think
we're using data volumes

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:58.640
to keep it persistent storage.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that was the next question

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:01.100
that I was going to ask.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:03.190
What happens if one of
those containers dies?

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:04.731
MARK MANDEL: So
right now, it resets.

00:16:04.731 --> 00:16:07.960
Yes, completely that's-- for
demo purposes, that's fine.

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:10.210
But yeah, if you were going
to run this as a real game

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:11.500
that people would
play all the time,

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:12.490
you would use data volumes.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:13.940
SANDEEP DINESH: Here's an
actual interesting fact.

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:15.880
I checked the life
of our cluster,

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:20.260
and it's pretty much been
the same containers running

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:23.056
for the past 200-plus days--

00:16:23.056 --> 00:16:23.930
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:25.555
SANDEEP DINESH:
--without any downtime.

00:16:25.555 --> 00:16:29.769
And I even upgraded the cluster
from 1.2 to 1.4, and it was--

00:16:29.769 --> 00:16:30.810
MARK MANDEL: It was fine.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:31.260
SANDEEP DINESH:
It was perfectly,

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:33.100
it just restarted the
pods, and it was all fine.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:34.120
That's amazing, yeah.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:35.995
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is pretty amazing, wow.

00:16:35.995 --> 00:16:38.980
So we've talked a little bit
about all the easy things,

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:42.500
like gRPC is amazing and
makes your life easier.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.900
Kubernetes makes it
scale very easily,

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:48.850
and containers, developing
on containers is so nice.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:50.220
What about the hard parts?

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:52.960
What is something
that you learned

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:54.880
or something that
you wish you knew

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:56.154
before you started doing this?

00:16:56.154 --> 00:16:57.820
SANDEEP DINESH: So
one interesting thing

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:01.370
is we used gRPC before
the 1.0 release.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:03.610
And it was much harder
to use back then.

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:05.859
I think there's a lot of bugs.

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:07.680
I talked to the team
being like, hey,

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.200
the Node.js package
doesn't compile.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:10.450
They're like, oh, don't worry.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:11.470
It'll compile next week.

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:13.660
And I'm like, OK, I'll
wait till next week.

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:15.810
And then it compiled
next week, which is fine.

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:18.177
With the 1.0, it's
much more stable.

00:17:18.177 --> 00:17:20.510
So I don't think people run
into those problems anymore.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.593
But it was a lot of fun
interacting with that team

00:17:22.593 --> 00:17:24.550
and really, hey, look,
we have a problem here.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.008
And then they're
immediately on it,

00:17:26.008 --> 00:17:27.220
which was a nice experience.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:29.710
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, at the
time, installation of protoc,

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.260
you had the compilers for
source and all that stuff.

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.420
