00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:26.210
I've known a lot
of people who I've

00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:27.670
known who have used
it and been huge fans.

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:28.770
And I never had a chance to
really, really play with it.

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:29.320
SANDEEP DINESH:
Yeah, this is cool.

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:31.880
MARK MANDEL: And I was
like, this is really cool.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:35.980
Yeah, really just being able
to manipulate data structures

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:38.752
online, or basically via an API.

00:22:38.752 --> 00:22:40.460
I don't have to worry
about how they work

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:43.220
and be able to access them from
multiple places was hugely,

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:44.002
hugely powerful.

00:22:44.002 --> 00:22:45.460
And yeah, like I
was talking about,

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.460
made matchmaking
super, super simple.

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:48.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, for me, one

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.760
of my favorite things
of Redis is the fact

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:55.190
that you have very simple
atomic operations, like append

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:58.766
to a list, which sounds obvious,
but once you can actually

00:22:58.766 --> 00:23:00.890
do that, everything changes,
because you don't need

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:05.890
to fetch the value, add one,
put back, making [INAUDIBLE],

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:07.690
same transaction,
stuff like that.

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:09.760
That is pretty awesome,
yep, that's true.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:13.420
So one of the clients
mentioned was a web client?

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:18.322
And as far as I know, gRPC
doesn't work for web stuff.

00:23:18.322 --> 00:23:19.780
SANDEEP DINESH:
That's right, yeah.

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:21.550
So that's actually
very interesting.

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:24.600
So gRPC does not
work on the web,

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:28.270
right, so it works on Node.js
JavaScript but not web

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:29.230
JavaScript.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:32.140
I think it's because
the browsers don't

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:35.800
expose the HTTP/2 functionality
to JavaScript yet.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:37.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is pretty impressive.

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:40.469
The browsers are the one
that don't support HTTP/2.

00:23:40.469 --> 00:23:42.760
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so the
JavaScript on the browsers

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:45.710
don't support the native HTTP/2
streaming and things like that.

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:48.020
It's necessary for gRPC.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:52.280
So the funny thing is we
had to build a gateway

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:55.674
to translate the gRPC
calls into web sockets.

00:23:55.674 --> 00:23:56.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:23:56.590 --> 00:23:58.089
SANDEEP DINESH: So
it's very simple.

00:23:58.089 --> 00:24:00.370
So I used Socket.IO
and a Node.js server.

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:02.450
And basically, whenever
the gRPC call came in,

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:05.250
I just convert it to a
WebSocket on Socket.IO,

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.800
send it to the front end that's
listening to that WebSocket

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.780
and then sending it back, right.

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:12.690
So just a little gateway.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.775
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
Socket.IO is a library?

00:24:14.775 --> 00:24:16.900
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so
it's a library for Node.js

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:21.460
that makes WebSockets
easier than without it.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:22.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:26.140
And you're running the library
as a standalone container,

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:27.629
or is it part of the server, or?

00:24:27.629 --> 00:24:29.920
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so this
is another client, right.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:32.380
So I'm running it on my
local machine, actually.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:34.690
And then just going to
localhost to run the game.

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:37.279
And you can easily
run this on the web

00:24:37.279 --> 00:24:38.320
and put it on the server.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:40.528
I think we could just make
it into another container.

00:24:40.528 --> 00:24:41.110
We're just--

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:41.926
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that'd be good.

00:24:41.926 --> 00:24:42.970
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, that'd
be a good thing to do.

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:44.350
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it actually
would be a really good thing

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:44.940
to do.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:46.481
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
would make sense.

00:24:46.481 --> 00:24:49.224
Otherwise, if I wanted
use it from my browser,

00:24:49.224 --> 00:24:50.140
I'd need to run also--

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:52.090
SANDEEP DINESH: Run Node.js
on your machine, yeah.

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:53.131
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, wow.

00:24:53.131 --> 00:24:56.051
OK, yeah, no, there's better
way to-- Are you sure?

00:24:56.051 --> 00:24:57.790
Oh, but that is an
interesting thing,

00:24:57.790 --> 00:25:00.710
to find that there's actually
already a-- basically a library

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:02.950
that you say, hey, this
is gRPC on one side.

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:03.450
This--

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:06.220
SANDEEP DINESH: Well, it's
not-- so I wrote it myself.

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:07.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Is
that open source, too?

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:09.058
SANDEEP DINESH: That
is also open source.

00:25:09.058 --> 00:25:11.160
It's on our repo.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:13.150
And it came up-- there
was part of the actual--

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:17.290
there's just one
monolithic node client.

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:18.880
And that did both
command line it.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:19.900
It did Arduino.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:20.815
And it did the web.

00:25:20.815 --> 00:25:22.190
And then when we
open sourced it,

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.130
we split it up into
smaller projects, which

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:26.170
is why we never got
the idea of running it

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:28.070
in a container on
Kubernetes, which

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:29.339
would make a lot of sense.

00:25:29.339 --> 00:25:30.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, yeah, I really

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:34.360
thought you would say, oh,
yeah, it runs on Kubernetes.

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:36.770
Would make sense, but OK, cool.

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:39.340
So we are running out of time,
but is there anything else,

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:41.530
any other topic that
we have not covered

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:42.950
that you'd like to mention?

00:25:42.950 --> 00:25:45.283
MARK MANDEL: I'll talk a
little bit about load balancing

00:25:45.283 --> 00:25:48.610
because it's a topic that
comes up with gRPC a lot.

00:25:48.610 --> 00:25:51.580
The nice thing about how
we implemented this for us

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:53.816
is that we have
short-lived games,

00:25:53.816 --> 00:25:55.190
and that's the
single connection.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:57.110
And once a game is
over, it disconnects.

00:25:57.110 --> 00:25:59.110
So there's things you can
do with load balancing

00:25:59.110 --> 00:26:00.901
with gRPC that can be
a little complicated,

00:26:00.901 --> 00:26:03.520
depending on what platform
you're on and how you do them.

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.410
But for us, it was very
simple because we're like, oh,

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:06.440
somebody's going to play a game.

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:08.950
Once the game is over, they'll
disconnect to the next person

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:09.565
who comes in.

00:26:09.565 --> 00:26:11.690
Or the time they want to
play, they reconnect again

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:13.294
and they can play
a whole new game,

00:26:13.294 --> 00:26:14.710
but it may be
different container.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:15.950
They're not stuck on
a single container

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:17.065
because you have a
single connection.

00:26:17.065 --> 00:26:17.590
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, I think--

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:19.256
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that is interesting.

00:26:19.256 --> 00:26:22.485
So what you're saying is that
if a container is running

00:26:22.485 --> 00:26:25.570
the game, if that
container died,

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:28.464
the connection would disappear,
so the game is interrupted.

00:26:28.464 --> 00:26:30.880
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, the game
would interrupt at that point.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:32.921
MARK MANDEL: Whenever you
tried to connect again,

00:26:32.921 --> 00:26:34.954
you would never go
to the wrong pod.

00:26:34.954 --> 00:26:36.370
You would just
connect to whatever

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:37.670
pod is running because
Kubernetes handles that.

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:38.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah yeah.

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:40.420
It connects to whichever pod,
and then it would matchmake you

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:41.159
all over again.

00:26:41.159 --> 00:26:42.950
And then you would
rejoin a whole new game.

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:43.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:46.030
Did you ever consider
trying to make it

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:51.970
so it actually if a
Docker container dies,

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:53.230
nothing bad happens?

00:26:53.230 --> 00:26:54.610
Is that even doable?

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:56.650
MARK MANDEL: In
theory, it was doable.

00:26:56.650 --> 00:26:58.420
You could store
the data in Redis.

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:00.500
I wanted to make it
as fast as possible,

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:02.367
and so I kept a lot
of it in memory.

00:27:02.367 --> 00:27:03.950
But in theory, you
could do that, too.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:04.950
That would work as well.

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:07.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: My question was
not only about the data itself

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:10.340
but also about
where-- how do you

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:13.529
handle that connection,
that streaming?

00:27:13.529 --> 00:27:15.820
Can you handle that stream
across different containers?

00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:17.611
MARK MANDEL: So right
now, the way it works

00:27:17.611 --> 00:27:19.510
is that connection,
it actually--

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:20.930
the way the game
is coded is such

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:23.316
that when that connection
declares that's the game,

00:27:23.316 --> 00:27:24.940
and as long as that
connection is live,

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:26.447
it makes things very simple.

00:27:26.447 --> 00:27:28.030
In theory, you could
also do something

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:30.545
like with every request, send
a game ID across the board.

00:27:30.545 --> 00:27:31.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Oh, interesting.

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:33.420
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
this is the game.

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:36.090
So if I'm attached to this,
this is the message I'm sending.

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:38.070
But it ended up being
simpler this way,

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:39.820
with a single connection,
because then you

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:41.470
knew if there was
something coming

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:43.770
up and down this particular
bidirectional stream,

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:45.440
we knew it had to
do with that game.

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:47.470
Otherwise, authentication
gets a little bit more tricky,

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.120
and there's a few
other security issues--

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:49.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, you're basically

00:27:49.990 --> 00:27:52.231
trading on a little
bit of flexibility

00:27:52.231 --> 00:27:58.690
on the how flexible it is to
errors and downtime to the fact

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.970
that it is super
comfortable to just use

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:02.710
a little bit of
statefulness there.

00:28:02.710 --> 00:28:03.980
And everything is so
much easier, cool.

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:04.580
MARK MANDEL: Everything
is much easier.

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:04.770
